course: dictionary
lesson: 608
title: Old-fashioned words that mean very
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound grown-up, old-fashioned, or a bit posh. «Awfully» is one of them. In old British books, characters say it all the time, so it helps to know what it does.
explain: The clues are in the words next to it. «Awfully kind» and «awfully sorry» both describe how someone feels or acts, and the word before «awfully» is a describing word. In «awfully kind», the kindness is strong, so awfully must mean very. Try the swap: «very kind» still makes sense, so you have found the meaning.
explain: «He did you awfully well!» snaps the Badger. Here awfully means very, so the Badger means he did you very well. In a few cases awfully means very badly, as in «He behaved awfully». Read the whole sentence to decide which meaning fits.
fact: «Awfully» means very or extremely.
fact: «Awfully» is informal and a little old-fashioned.
fact: In modern speech, «awfully» is a polite, slightly posh way to say «very».
fact: «Awfully» can also mean very badly, as in «He behaved awfully».
fact: Test a meaning by swapping in a simple word: «awfully kind» becomes «very kind», so the meaning is very.
exercise: «He was awfully sorry for being late.» In this sentence, awfully means | *Very; extremely | Not at all | Very badly | Quietly
exercise: «He behaved awfully.» What does awfully mean here? | Very kindly | Very politely | *Very badly | Very quickly
exercise: Which word could you swap for awfully in «awfully nice» without changing the meaning? | Quietly | *Very | Badly | Slowly
test: «It was awfully cold this morning.» What does awfully mean? | Badly | Quietly | Sadly | *Very, extremely
test: Which best describes awfully in modern speech? | A slang word for bad | Another word for terrible | *A polite, slightly posh way to say very | A word only used in Scotland
exam: «He did you awfully well!» snaps the Badger. In this sentence, awfully means | Badly | Sadly | *Very | Quietly
